WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.589
Lesson five.

00:00:01.589 --> 00:00:02.828
Generations.

00:00:02.828 --> 00:00:07.651
Now let's watch the population improve, one generation at a time.

00:00:08.251 --> 00:00:10.193
Each dot is one pilot.

00:00:10.193 --> 00:00:16.279
Each column is one generation of fifty pilots, and the height of a dot is its fitness.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:20.463
Red pilots mostly crash, and green ones mostly land.

00:00:21.063 --> 00:00:25.099
In the first thirty generations, nearly every pilot crashes.

00:00:25.099 --> 00:00:31.391
But each generation is sampled around the best of the last one, so the whole column creeps upward.

00:00:31.391 --> 00:00:35.965
The best pilot lands half its episodes by generation thirty-two.

00:00:36.565 --> 00:00:38.967
Then the whole population climbs.

00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:42.247
By generation fifty, a fifth of the pilots land.

00:00:42.247 --> 00:00:44.649
By seventy-five, most of them do.

00:00:44.649 --> 00:00:49.101
The orange line is the best pilot, and the blue line is the average.

00:00:49.101 --> 00:00:54.498
As the population agrees on one strategy, the average catches up with the best.

00:00:55.098 --> 00:00:58.822
Fitness is just a number, so let's watch the flights.

00:00:58.822 --> 00:01:06.631
Every pilot of a generation starts from exactly the same spot, with the same push, and we draw where each one goes.

00:01:07.231 --> 00:01:09.245
Generation zero is chaos.

00:01:09.245 --> 00:01:13.884
Pilots fall straight down, or fire an engine and fly off the screen.

00:01:13.884 --> 00:01:20.227
One generation later, and then five, the paths change shape, but not one of them lands yet.

00:01:20.827 --> 00:01:24.375
By generation ten, a few paths bend toward the pad.

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:29.065
Through twenty and thirty, the paths tighten, but only a handful land.

00:01:29.065 --> 00:01:30.809
By forty, eight land.

00:01:30.809 --> 00:01:34.476
And by fifty, nineteen of the fifty pilots land.

00:01:35.076 --> 00:01:37.220
By seventy-five, forty land.

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.364
By one hundred, forty-eight.

00:01:39.364 --> 00:01:44.310
In the end, the trails bundle into one smooth curve between the flags.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.151
This is the same population, seen as points.

00:01:48.151 --> 00:01:50.707
Each pilot is thirty-six numbers.

00:01:50.707 --> 00:01:57.801
A method called principal component analysis flattens them down to two, so each dot is a whole pilot.

00:01:58.401 --> 00:02:05.418
The centre of the cloud, in blue, travels a long way from where it started, toward the region where pilots land.

00:02:05.418 --> 00:02:07.765
The white line is its path.

00:02:08.365 --> 00:02:10.025
Here is a neat trick.

00:02:10.025 --> 00:02:15.805
Only the highest score matters, so if you double every weight, every decision stays the same.

00:02:15.805 --> 00:02:21.528
As the weights grow, the same step size becomes a smaller and smaller step relative to them.

00:02:21.528 --> 00:02:24.861
The search turns into fine-tuning on its own.
